# =============================================================================
#  What must never reach the repository
# =============================================================================
#  Two separate concerns, and the second is the dangerous one:
#
#    1. Noise    — dependencies, build output, editor files. Committing these
#                  is untidy and makes diffs unreadable, but nothing worse.
#
#    2. SECRETS AND PEOPLE'S DATA — environment files, database dumps, keys.
#                  A leak here is not untidy, it is a breach: volunteer names,
#                  emails, password hashes and two-factor secrets. And git
#                  keeps history, so a file committed once stays recoverable
#                  even after it is deleted. The only reliable protection is
#                  never committing it in the first place.
#
#  Check before committing anything unfamiliar:   git status
#  Ask why a specific file is or is not ignored:  git check-ignore -v <path>
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SECRETS  — the section that matters
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Environment files. `.env.example` is the tracked template and is the ONLY one
# that may ever be committed; it contains placeholders, never real values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Certificates and private keys, wherever they land.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  DATABASE DUMPS  — these contain every volunteer's data
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  docs/04-LAUNCH-ROADMAP.md tells you to take backups with pg_dump, and the
#  obvious place to run that is the project root. A dump holds every user row:
#  names, emails, password hashes, encrypted 2FA secrets, and the station's
#  whole equipment history. It must never be committed.
#
#  NOTE the deliberate narrowness: `*.sql` is NOT ignored, because Prisma's
#  migrations are .sql files and they MUST be tracked — they are how the
#  database is rebuilt. Only backup-shaped names are excluded.
*.dump
*.backup
*.bak
backup/
backups/
dumps/
lrc401-*.sql
*-backup-*.sql
pg_dump*

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  DEPENDENCIES AND BUILD OUTPUT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

client/dist/
server/dist/
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Prisma's generated client — rebuilt by `prisma generate` on every install.
# prisma/migrations is NOT ignored: it is the schema's history and is required.
**/.prisma/
server/prisma/generated/
server/prisma/*.db
server/prisma/*.db-journal

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  RUNTIME AND LOCAL TOOLING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
logs/

# Files uploaded by users at runtime — station data, not source.
server/uploads/

# Portable runtimes kept in the project rather than in ~/tools.
tools/
*.msi
*.exe

# Test and coverage artefacts.
coverage/
.nyc_output/
*.lcov

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  EDITOR AND OS NOISE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .vscode is not ignored wholesale: shared recommendations and launch configs
# are genuinely useful to a future maintainer. Only personal settings are.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Agent/session scratch files, if any tool writes them into the project.
.claude/
*.scratch
__*-probe.*
.vercel
.env*
